# Day 58 Content Generator: Enterprise DevSecOps & Software Supply Chain Security

DAY_58 = {'day': 58, 'title': 'Enterprise DevSecOps & Software Supply Chain Security: SLSA Level 4, Cosign, Kyverno & SBOMs', 'goal': 'Architect and enforce an enterprise-grade cryptographic software supply chain security pipeline. Implement automated Software Bill of Materials (SBOM) generation with Syft, vulnerability scanning with Grype, keyless container signing with Sigstore Cosign, admission governance with Kyverno, and SLSA Level 4 build provenance.', 'minutes': 45, 'recap': 'Across Days 56 and 57, we mastered multi-cloud control planes with Crossplane and advanced cloud financial operations with Karpenter and Kubecost. Today on Day 58, we defend the enterprise against the single most devastating attack vector in modern technology: software supply chain poisoning and malicious dependency injection.', 'parts': [{'part': 1, 'title': 'The Modern Supply Chain Threat: SolarWinds, Dependency Hijacking & Cryptographic Attestation', 'say': ["Welcome to Day 58 of Senior DevOps Engineering and GitOps. Today, we confront the most insidious and dangerous security frontier in modern computing: Software Supply Chain Security. For decades, cybersecurity focused almost exclusively on perimeter defense: building firewalls around datacenters, securing network ports, and enforcing strong passwords. But in recent years, sophisticated nation-state adversaries realized a chilling vulnerability: they do not need to penetrate a corporation's heavily fortified production firewall if they can quietly poison the software before it is ever deployed.", "To understand how software supply chain attacks function, consider an everyday physical analogy: a municipal pharmaceutical factory that manufactures essential medicines. The factory building itself has biometric security guards, retinal scanners, and armored vault doors. But the factory does not synthesize every chemical molecule from raw dirt; it purchases bulk chemical ingredients from global suppliers—sugar binders, gelatin capsules, and active chemical compounds. If a hostile infiltrator taints a drum of gelatin at a supplier's warehouse thousands of miles away, the factory innocently mixes the poisoned ingredient into millions of pills, stamps them with its official corporate seal of approval, and distributes them to hospitals worldwide. The poison entered through the trusted supply chain.", "In software engineering, this nightmare became reality in historic cyberattacks like SolarWinds and the XZ Utils backdoor. In the SolarWinds attack, Russian state hackers did not exploit a bug in production servers. They compromised the internal build system of the software company itself, injecting malicious code into Orion software updates while the code was compiling. SolarWinds digitally signed the poisoned update with their official corporate certificate and distributed it to eighteen thousand enterprise and government customers, including the Pentagon, the US Treasury Department, and Fortune 500 corporations. The customers' firewalls permitted the malicious payload because it was signed by a trusted vendor.", "Modern cloud applications are composed of over ninety percent open-source dependencies. When a developer writes npm install, pip install, or go get, they pull in thousands of transitive third-party packages authored by anonymous maintainers around the globe. If an attacker hijacks a maintainer's GitHub account or publishes a malicious typosquatted package, your production Kubernetes clusters ingest that compromised code within hours through automated CI/CD pipelines.", "To defeat this existential threat, the tech industry formulated the Supply-chain Levels for Software Artifacts (SLSA, pronounced 'salsa') framework. Created by Google and the OpenSSF, SLSA defines a formal, non-negotiable maturity model across four levels. SLSA guarantees that software is authored in audited source control, built by ephemeral, isolated build platforms with zero human interference, and verified by cryptographic build provenance attestations that prove precisely who built the container image, from which commit SHA, and using which exact compiler parameters.", 'By mastering SLSA principles and cryptographic supply chain defenses, you ensure that every container running in your production environment carries an immutable, cryptographically verifiable pedigree, closing the door on supply chain sabotage.', 'To appreciate the scale and stealth of software supply chain threats, consider the chilling mechanics of the XZ Utils backdoor discovered in early 2024. In that operation, a state-sponsored persona spent over two years establishing credibility as a helpful open-source volunteer, gradually taking over maintainership of a critical compression utility embedded in nearly every Linux server on Earth. Over months of subtle commits, the attacker injected obfuscated, multi-stage binary test payloads that hooked into the OpenSSH daemon during system compilation, effectively creating an undetectable master skeleton key into global Linux servers. Had an observant database engineer not noticed a tiny five-hundred-millisecond CPU delay during local SSH login profiling, the backdoor would have silently rolled into every major Linux distribution worldwide.', "This terrifying incident permanently destroyed the naive assumption that open-source software is inherently secure simply because 'many eyeballs make all bugs shallow'. In reality, millions of complex open-source libraries are maintained by solitary, exhausted volunteers working in their spare time without corporate security budgets. Software supply chain security is the urgent recognition that modern software development is not merely an engineering process, but a high-stakes manufacturing pipeline where every single component, screw, and raw material must be tested, attested, and validated against hostile tampering before assembly."], 'check': {'question': 'What is the primary architectural objective of the SLSA (Supply-chain Levels for Software Artifacts) framework?', 'options': ['To establish cryptographic provenance that verifies code was built in an isolated, tamper-proof environment from an audited source commit, preventing malicious supply chain poisoning', 'To calculate the total retail price of software licenses sold to consumers', 'To permanently remove all open-source libraries from the internet', 'To replace container images with raw uncompiled assembly code'], 'answerIndex': 0, 'why': 'SLSA establishes a rigorous security framework that cryptographically verifies software origin, build isolation, and provenance to prevent supply chain tampering.'}}, {'part': 2, 'title': 'Software Bill of Materials (SBOM) Generation: Syft, CycloneDX, SPDX & Grype Vulnerability Scanners', 'say': ['The foundational cornerstone of supply chain defense is comprehensive visibility: you cannot defend what you cannot see. In the pharmaceutical industry, every box of medicine must display an FDA ingredients label listing every active and inactive chemical compound. In software engineering, this mandatory ingredient label is known as the Software Bill of Materials, or SBOM.', 'An SBOM is a machine-readable, cryptographically verifiable inventory listing every library, package, compiler dependency, and operating system package bundled inside a container image. Two standardized open formats dominate the industry: SPDX (Software Package Data Exchange), maintained by the Linux Foundation, and CycloneDX, maintained by OWASP. Both formats describe the exact package names, versions, license types, and cryptographic SHA-256 hashes of every file in the application.', 'To generate SBOMs automatically in our CI/CD pipeline, we utilize open-source tools like Anchore Syft. When our GitHub Actions or GitLab CI runner finishes building an OCI container image, Syft scans the image layers, inspecting Linux package managers (such as dpkg, rpm, and apk) as well as language-level lockfiles (such as package-lock.json, go.mod, and Cargo.lock). In seconds, Syft outputs a standardized CycloneDX JSON SBOM documenting every dependency.', 'Next in the pipeline is Vulnerability Scanning with Anchore Grype or Aqua Trivy. Rather than performing naive regex scans, Grype takes the generated SBOM and compares it against continuously updated vulnerability databases, including the National Vulnerability Database (NVD), GitHub Security Advisories (GHSA), and Linux distribution security trackers. Grype identifies Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVEs), rating their severity using the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS).', 'Crucially, our CI pipeline enforces automated Vulnerability Gate Policies. If Grype identifies a Critical or High CVE with a known upstream fix (such as an unpatched OpenSSL remote code execution vulnerability), the CI pipeline automatically fails the build, blocking the container from ever being pushed to the container registry.', 'Furthermore, we store the generated SBOM alongside the container image inside our OCI registry. Modern OCI registries (such as AWS ECR, Google Artifact Registry, and Harbor) support OCI artifact specifications, allowing the SBOM to be attached directly to the container digest. When an auditor or security engineer inspects a running pod in Kubernetes, they can query the registry to retrieve the exact SBOM that was generated at build time.', 'By institutionalizing automated SBOM generation and Grype vulnerability scanning, you transform container images from opaque, mysterious black boxes into transparent, fully audited digital packages.', 'To understand how SBOMs transform incident response, consider what happened across the global technology industry during the historic Log4j (Log4Shell) crisis in December 2021. When a remote code execution vulnerability was disclosed in a ubiquitous Java logging utility, enterprise security teams descended into blind panic. Thousands of engineering teams had no idea which of their hundreds of microservices used Java, let alone which specific services bundled the vulnerable Log4j library deep inside nested JAR files. SREs spent weeks manually grepping through source code repositories, running uncoordinated scans, and rebooting production servers.', "In organizations that had implemented automated SBOM pipelines, the response was radically different. Security teams executed a single automated SQL query across their central SBOM database: SELECT service_name, container_image FROM sbom_inventory WHERE package_name = 'log4j-core' AND version < '2.17.1'. Within thirty seconds, the enterprise had an exhaustive, complete list of exactly which seven microservices were vulnerable out of thousands of running workloads. Patches were applied, images recompiled, and new signed containers deployed to Kubernetes within hours. An SBOM transforms incident response from frantic guesswork into deterministic, automated precision.", 'Furthermore, SBOMs solve the critical legal challenge of open-source license compliance. In commercial software, accidentally bundling a library licensed under strict GNU General Public License (GPL) terms can legally force a corporation to open-source its proprietary intellectual property. Syft and Grype inspect license headers automatically, alerting compliance teams whenever a non-permissive license (such as AGPL or GPLv3) is introduced into commercial container builds.'], 'check': {'question': 'What is the primary function of a Software Bill of Materials (SBOM) in modern container security?', 'options': ['It provides a comprehensive, machine-readable inventory of all third-party libraries, packages, and OS dependencies inside a container image to enable vulnerability tracking and license compliance', 'It prints out physical invoices to charge software developers for each line of code written', 'It converts container images into compressed MP3 audio files', 'It automatically deletes all open-source packages from the production servers'], 'answerIndex': 0, 'why': 'An SBOM serves as an immutable ingredient list of all dependencies within a container, enabling security teams to instantly identify affected workloads when new CVEs are published.'}}, {'part': 3, 'title': 'Cryptographic Artifact Signing with Sigstore Cosign: Keyless Signing, Fulcio & Rekor Transparency Log', 'say': ['Generating an SBOM and scanning for vulnerabilities is vital, but how does a production Kubernetes cluster know that the container image it pulls from the registry is the exact binary that passed your CI security scans, and not a malicious image pushed by an attacker who compromised your Docker Hub credentials? The answer is Cryptographic Artifact Signing with Sigstore Cosign.', 'For years, cryptographic signing of software was a notoriously painful administrative nightmare. Organizations had to manage private PGP or GPG keys, store them on physical hardware USB dongles (like YubiKeys), and worry constantly about key rotation, key loss, or private key leakage. If a developer left the company, private keys had to be revoked and re-issued. This friction caused ninety-nine percent of organizations to skip artifact signing entirely.', 'The Sigstore project, backed by Google, Red Hat, and the Linux Foundation, revolutionized artifact signing by introducing Keyless Signing. Keyless signing eliminates long-lived private keys completely, replacing them with short-lived certificates tied to OpenID Connect (OIDC) identities.', 'Let us trace the architectural flow of Keyless Signing using Cosign, Fulcio, and Rekor. When our CI/CD pipeline (such as GitHub Actions) finishes building and scanning a container image, the Cosign CLI requests an OIDC identity token from the CI environment. This token proves cryptographically that the build job is running in a specific repository, on a specific branch, triggered by a specific commit.', "Next, Cosign sends this OIDC token to Fulcio, Sigstore's free, automated Certificate Authority. Fulcio verifies the OIDC token and issues a short-lived X.509 digital certificate valid for only ten minutes. The certificate contains no personal passwords; it embeds the repository URL and Git commit SHA directly inside the Subject Alternative Name (SAN). Cosign signs the container image digest using the temporary private key corresponding to this certificate.", "Simultaneously, Cosign submits the cryptographic signature and certificate to Rekor, Sigstore's immutable, append-only transparency log. Rekor operates like a public blockchain for software security: once a signature is recorded in Rekor's Merkle tree, it is mathematically impossible to alter, forge, or delete. After the signature is logged in Rekor, the temporary private key is immediately discarded and destroyed.", "Now, anyone in the world—or any Kubernetes cluster—can verify the container's authenticity using Cosign. The verifier queries Rekor to confirm that the container was signed by a legitimate Fulcio certificate during its ten-minute validity window by the authorized GitHub Actions workflow. You achieve absolute cryptographic non-repudiation with zero static key management.", 'Mastering Sigstore Cosign establishes an unbreakable cryptographic chain of custody from source commit to production deployment.', "To understand why Rekor's transparency log is such a revolutionary innovation in public cryptography, consider the historical failure of traditional Certificate Authorities (CAs). In the past, if a rogue hacker or rogue government compromised a commercial Certificate Authority, they could secretly forge an SSL certificate for google.com or bankofamerica.com and conduct man-in-the-middle attacks without anyone knowing until victims complained months later. In response, the web created Certificate Transparency (CT) logs: public, append-only ledgers where every single issued certificate must be broadcast publicly for all the world to inspect.", 'Sigstore applies this exact transparent ledger architecture to software binaries through Rekor. Rekor is built on a cryptographic Merkle tree—the exact same mathematical data structure that underpins distributed blockchain ledgers. When Cosign records a signature in Rekor, Rekor generates a Signed Entry Timestamp (SET) and an inclusion proof. Anyone can verify that the signature was recorded in the public ledger without trusting Sigstore or the signer. If a rogue actor attempted to forge a signature, the forgery would be immediately visible on the public transparency log, triggering immediate global alarms.', 'Moreover, Cosign supports Attestation Signing. Cosign does not sign only container images; it can sign any arbitrary metadata document—including Syft SBOMs, Grype vulnerability scan reports, and SLSA provenance manifests. These signed attestations are stored in the OCI registry as OCI referrers. When a container image moves through testing, staging, and production environments, its complete cryptographic medical record travels with it, permanently bound to the container digest.'], 'check': {'question': "How does Sigstore Cosign's 'Keyless Signing' mechanism achieve cryptographic container signing without managing long-lived private keys?", 'options': ['It exchanges CI/CD OIDC identity tokens for short-lived 10-minute X.509 certificates from Fulcio and records the cryptographic signature in the Rekor public transparency log before discarding the private key', 'It stores static passwords in unencrypted plain text files on GitHub public repositories', 'It asks human developers to sign physical paper forms and fax them to datacenter administrators', 'It disables digital encryption entirely so that containers require no verification'], 'answerIndex': 0, 'why': "Keyless signing leverages ephemeral certificates from Fulcio bound to OIDC build identities and recorded in Rekor's transparency log, eliminating the operational hazards of static private key management."}}, {'part': 4, 'title': 'Policy Enforcement at Admission Time: Kyverno vs OPA Gatekeeper for Supply Chain Verification', 'say': ['Signing containers in CI is meaningless if your production Kubernetes cluster permits unsigned, unverified containers to run. If a cluster administrator or an attacker compromises a namespace and runs kubectl run rogue-miner --image=malicious/image:latest, what prevents Kubernetes from downloading and executing that hostile container? The answer is Admission Controller Policy Enforcement using Kyverno or Open Policy Agent (OPA) Gatekeeper.', "In modern Kubernetes, the Dynamic Admission Control webhook is the ultimate security checkpoint. Whenever a pod creation request is sent to the Kubernetes API server, the API server pauses the request and forwards the raw manifest to admission webhooks before committing the object to etcd. If an admission policy webhook rejects the request, the pod is never created, and the container image is never pulled by the node's container runtime.", 'Two dominant policy engines govern cloud-native admission: Open Policy Agent (OPA) Gatekeeper and Kyverno. OPA Gatekeeper is an extremely powerful, general-purpose policy engine that evaluates policies written in Rego, a declarative query language. However, Rego has a steep learning curve and requires developers to master custom domain-specific syntax. Kyverno, in contrast, was designed natively for Kubernetes: Kyverno policies are written in pure, standard Kubernetes YAML without requiring any custom programming language.', 'Let us examine how a production Kyverno policy enforces supply chain verification. We deploy a Kyverno ClusterPolicy in Enforce mode targeting Pods. The policy spec uses the verifyImages rule. Inside this rule, we specify our authorized container registry and define the cryptographic attestation requirements: we mandate that the image must be signed by Sigstore Cosign, matching our corporate OIDC issuer and repository subject.', "When a developer attempts to deploy an image, Kyverno intercepts the pod creation request. In less than fifty milliseconds, Kyverno contacts Rekor and the container registry, downloads the cryptographic signature, and validates the digital signature against Fulcio's root certificate. If the image signature is missing, forged, or belongs to an unauthorized repository, Kyverno instantly rejects the request with an explicit rejection error: Image failed Sigstore Cosign signature verification.", 'Furthermore, Kyverno can verify attached attestations: confirming that an SBOM was generated by Syft, that Grype reported zero Critical CVEs, and that the SLSA build provenance was generated by an authorized GitHub runner. Any container that fails any of these cryptographic criteria is physically blocked from entering the cluster.', 'By deploying Kyverno admission enforcement, you establish an automated cryptographic gatekeeper at the perimeter of your Kubernetes cluster, guaranteeing that only audited, signed, and certified code can ever run in production.', 'To appreciate the elegance of Kyverno in enterprise Kubernetes, examine how it compares to legacy perimeter security tools. In traditional architectures, security teams relied on static image scanners that scanned container registries once a week. But a weekly scan is completely blind to new zero-day vulnerabilities published on Tuesday morning, and powerless to stop a developer from deploying an unvetted image directly from an untrusted public registry. Kyverno brings enforcement directly to the point of execution: the Kubernetes admission controller.', "Consider the detailed mechanics of Kyverno's verifyImages rule during a deployment. When a deployment manifest arrives, Kyverno does not simply check whether the image tag exists. Kyverno extracts the immutable SHA-256 digest of the image: for example, image@sha256:7b49... Next, Kyverno queries Sigstore's Rekor log to verify the cryptographic signature. It checks the Subject Alternative Name (SAN) inside the Fulcio certificate to ensure the image was built from your specific corporate GitHub organization (such as https://github.com/pinit-enterprise/*), on the approved main branch, and signed by GitHub Actions' official OIDC token issuer (https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com).", "If an attacker somehow gained write access to your container registry and uploaded a backdoored image with the tag v1.4.0, but that image was built on the attacker's personal laptop rather than your authorized GitHub Actions pipeline, the attacker's image will lack a valid Fulcio certificate and Rekor entry. Kyverno will ruthlessly reject the deployment at admission time, logging a security audit event and blocking the poisoned container from ever touching your production Kubernetes cluster."], 'check': {'question': "What happens when a developer attempts to deploy an unsigned container image into a Kubernetes cluster governed by a Kyverno 'verifyImages' policy?", 'options': ["Kyverno's admission webhook intercepts the API request, fails the cryptographic signature verification, and rejects pod creation before the container image is ever downloaded to worker nodes", 'Kyverno downloads the container and runs it with root privileges anyway', "Kyverno charges an administrative penalty fee to the developer's credit card", 'The Kubernetes cluster permanently deletes all worker nodes'], 'answerIndex': 0, 'why': 'Kyverno evaluates image signatures at admission time; if an image lacks a valid signature from an authorized signer, the webhook rejects the creation request, preventing the image from ever being pulled.'}}, {'part': 5, 'title': 'Production DevSecOps Invariants: SLSA Level 4 Provenance, Ephemeral Build Runners & Zero-Trust CI', 'say': ['We culminate Day 58 by formalizing the non-negotiable Production DevSecOps Invariants required to achieve SLSA Level 4 supply chain certification. Operating enterprise software delivery at the highest level of security maturity demands an uncompromising defense-in-depth posture across the entire build lifecycle.', 'Invariant 1 is Ephemeral, Isolated Build Runners. Traditional CI/CD architectures where build jobs run on persistent, static virtual machines or long-lived Jenkins worker servers represent an extreme vulnerability. If a malicious build script compromises the host filesystem, it can persist rootkits, hijack subsequent builds, and steal secrets belonging to other projects. In an enterprise SLSA Level 4 pipeline, all build jobs execute on single-use, ephemeral runners (such as GitHub Actions hosted runners or Actions Runner Controller pods) that are spun up dynamically from pristine, immutable images and completely destroyed immediately after a single job completes. No state or residual files persist between builds.', 'Invariant 2 is Two-Person Code Review and Hermetic Builds. Under SLSA Level 4, no individual software engineer—regardless of their executive rank or seniority—is permitted to unilaterally push code directly into production. All source changes must pass through pull requests with mandatory two-person cryptographic approvals recorded in signed Git commits. Furthermore, build environments must be Hermetic: build steps execute in isolated network sandboxes where external internet access is disabled, guaranteeing that build scripts cannot download unpinned, rogue dependencies during the compilation phase.', 'Invariant 3 is Immutable In-Toto Attestations for SLSA Provenance. When a container image is compiled, our CI pipeline generates an in-toto formatted predicate attestation: a machine-readable document declaring the exact repository, commit SHA, build workflow, build parameters, and cryptographic SHA-256 digests of all input artifacts. This provenance document is signed with Cosign and pushed directly to the OCI registry alongside the image.', 'Invariant 4 is Continuous Runtime Drift Detection with NeuVector and Falco. While admission controllers verify images when pods are created, attackers could theoretically attempt in-memory code injection or container escape after deployment. We deploy Sysdig Falco and SUSE NeuVector to monitor Linux kernel system calls in real time. If a running container suddenly executes an unauthorized shell (such as /bin/sh), modifies an immutable binary, or opens an unauthorized outbound socket to an unknown IP address, Falco emits immediate security alerts, and the automated runtime controller kills the compromised pod instantly.', "Adhering to these four production DevSecOps invariants ensures that your enterprise software delivery pipeline satisfies the world's most stringent regulatory standards, protecting customer data and corporate integrity against the most sophisticated cyber adversaries.", 'Let us examine how Hermetic Builds and Two-Person Code Review establish an unbreakable security baseline under SLSA Level 4. In a hermetic build, the compiler is executed inside an isolated sandbox container with all external network routing disabled at the Linux network namespace level. All dependencies—the compiler toolchain, third-party libraries, and configuration files—must be pre-fetched, cryptographically verified against pinned SHA-256 hashes in lockfiles, and mounted locally as read-only volumes before the build process begins. This guarantees that a build script cannot make unauthorized outbound network connections to fetch unvetted code during compilation.', 'Furthermore, SLSA Level 4 requires strict non-falsifiable provenance. This means the metadata document describing how the software was built cannot be generated or modified by the build script itself. If a malicious developer authors a compromised build script, that script could easily forge a provenance report claiming the software was clean. To prevent this, the provenance attestation is generated and signed by the trusted build orchestrator (such as the GitHub Actions platform itself) outside the execution boundary of the user-controlled container.', 'Finally, examine Sysdig Falco in-kernel runtime defense. Even if an image is completely clean and signed at deploy time, an attacker might discover a remote code execution vulnerability in the running application months later. Falco leverages Linux eBPF probes hooked directly into kernel system calls (such as execve, openat, and socket). If a hijacked web server suddenly spawns a bash shell or writes to /etc/shadow, Falco intercepts the system call in microseconds, terminates the process, and alerts the SRE security team. You achieve complete 360-degree security: signed provenance at build time, strict admission gating at deploy time, and in-kernel eBPF defense at runtime.'], 'check': {'question': 'Why are single-use ephemeral build runners strictly required to achieve SLSA Level 4 software supply chain security?', 'options': ['Ephemeral runners ensure that build environments are spun up cleanly and completely destroyed after every job, preventing persistent malware, cross-build contamination, and secret leakage', 'Ephemeral runners reduce cloud costs by only running for three seconds per week', 'Ephemeral runners eliminate the need for computer monitors in software offices', 'Ephemeral runners allow developers to bypass Git version control completely'], 'answerIndex': 0, 'why': 'Single-use ephemeral runners guarantee total isolation between builds, ensuring that no malicious payload or residual state can carry over from one build execution to the next.'}}], 'summary': ['Day 58 defends the enterprise software supply chain against nation-state attacks by implementing SLSA Level 4 cryptographic standards.', 'Anchore Syft generates standardized CycloneDX and SPDX Software Bills of Materials (SBOMs), while Grype scans for CVE vulnerabilities.', 'Sigstore Cosign provides keyless cryptographic container signing using ephemeral Fulcio X.509 certificates and the Rekor transparency log.', 'Kyverno admission controllers enforce verifyImages policies at admission time, physically blocking unsigned or unvetted containers from Kubernetes.', 'Production invariants mandate single-use ephemeral build runners, hermetic compilation, signed in-toto provenance attestations, and Falco runtime defense.'], 'projectStep': {'title': 'Block 12 Day 58: Enterprise DevSecOps & Supply Chain Security', 'steps': ['Configure GitHub Actions to generate a CycloneDX SBOM using Anchore Syft during container builds.', 'Integrate Anchore Grype to scan container images and fail the CI build on Critical/High CVEs.', 'Implement keyless container signing with Sigstore Cosign, authenticating via GitHub Actions OIDC and logging to Rekor.', 'Deploy a Kyverno ClusterPolicy in Kubernetes enforcing signature and SBOM verification for all production pods.']}}
